[require map::point::store::fs [opt [vset VERSION]]]
[description]

This package provides a store of [term geo/point] definitions, loaded from the [term geo/point]
files (see [package map::point::file]) found in a directory of the filesystem.

[para] The store is a basic example of a [term geo/point] store, and defines the
[sectref {Store API}] expected from the backing store of a [package map::point::store::memory]. It
can also be used directly with [package map::point::table-display].

[section API]

[list_begin definitions]

[call [cmd ::map::point::store::fs] [arg storeName] [arg directory] [opt [arg options]...]]

Creates a new store object named [arg storeName], and returns the fully qualified name of its
command. The [arg storeName] can be [const %AUTO%] to generate a unique name.

[para] All files in [arg directory] matching the [option -pattern] are read with
[cmd {map point file read}]. Files which cannot be read, or are not valid [term geo/point] files,
are ignored. Subdirectories are not searched. The files are read only once, at creation, later
changes to the directory are not seen by the store.

[para] The only option is

[list_begin options]
[opt_def -pattern [arg pattern]]
The glob pattern selecting the files to read in [arg directory]. Defaults to [const *.points]. This
option is read-only, it can only be set at creation.
[list_end]

[call [arg storeName] [method ids]]

Returns a list of the ids of all [term geo/point]s in the store, in dictionary order. As a file can
contain several points, the id of a [term geo/point] is the path of the file it was read from
([arg directory] joined with the name of the file), followed by a slash and the position of the
point in the file, starting at [const 1]. For example, [const /data/cafes.points/3] is the third
point of file [file /data/cafes.points].

[call [arg storeName] [method get] [arg id]]

Returns the [term geo/point] with the given [arg id], a dictionary with keys [const names],
[const kind], and [const geo], as described in [package map::point::file]. Throws an error if the
[arg id] is not known.

[call [arg storeName] [method visible] [arg geobox] [arg zoom]]

Returns a list of the ids of all [term geo/point]s located inside the geographical box
[arg geobox] (a list of minimal latitude, minimal longitude, maximal latitude and maximal
longitude). The [arg zoom] level is ignored.

[call [arg storeName] [method cget] [arg option]]

Returns the value of the [arg option].

[call [arg storeName] [method destroy]]

Destroys the store.

[list_end]

[section {Store API}]

The methods [method ids] and [method get] described above are the API of a [term geo/point] store
used by [package map::point::store::memory] to access its backing store, and by
[package map::point::table-display]. Any object providing these methods can be used in place of
[package map::point::store::fs], for example a store backed by a database. Such a store must
satisfy:

[list_begin definitions]
[def "[arg store] [method ids]"]
Returns a list of the ids of all [term geo/point]s in the store. The ids are arbitrary strings,
unique within the store. They should not start with [const c/], which
[package map::point::store::memory] uses for the ids of the clusters it creates.

[def "[arg store] [method get] [arg id]"]
Returns the [term geo/point] with the given [arg id], a dictionary containing at least the keys
[const names] (a list of names), [const kind] (a string), and [const geo] (a list of latitude and
longitude), see [package map::point::file]. Further keys are allowed, and are passed through by
[package map::point::store::memory].
[list_end]

[para] The method [method visible] is not used by [package map::point::store::memory], which
computes the visible points itself.

[para] The store must be given to the users as the name of a single command, a command prefix with
arguments is not supported.

[section EXAMPLE]

[example_begin]
package require map::point::store::fs

map point store fs Points /path/to/directory

foreach id [lb]Points ids[rb] {
    set point [lb]Points get $id[rb]
    dict with point {
        puts "$id: [lb]join $names {, }[rb] ($kind) at $geo"
    }
}

# The points in the area of Vancouver
puts [lb]Points visible {49.19 -123.27 49.32 -123.02} 0[rb]
[example_end]

